## The Bug

The goroutine captures the **variable** `i` by reference, not its current value. All five goroutines share the same `i`. By the time any of them actually runs, the `for` loop has already completed and `i` equals `5`.

**Offending line:**
```go
go func() {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)  // i is read at execution time, not at launch time
}()
```

## The Fix

Pass `i` as a parameter to the goroutine so each one gets its own copy:

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(j int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			fmt.Println(j)
		}(i)
	}
	wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

Each goroutine now receives its own `int` parameter `j`, which is a fresh value copied at the time the goroutine is launched. The output will be `0 1 2 3 4` (in some order, since goroutines run concurrently).

